// 3-qubit bit-flip code — the simplest real quantum error correction code.
// Encodes one logical qubit across 3 physical ones (d0,d1,d2), so a single
// bit-flip error on any one of them can be detected and corrected without
// ever measuring the data qubits directly (which would destroy the state
// this is meant to protect).
//
// Two ancillas (a0,a1) measure parity, not the data itself: a0 compares
// d0/d1, a1 compares d1/d2. Which one flips tells you which data qubit is
// wrong, the same way two overlapping checks can locate a single error.

qubit d0, d1, d2, a0, a1

// Encode a logical |1> across all 3 data qubits.
X d0
CNOT d0 d1
CNOT d0 d2

// A real bit-flip error would go here, e.g. X 1 to flip d1.

// Syndrome extraction — parity checks, not direct measurement.
CNOT d0 a0
CNOT d1 a0
CNOT d1 a1
CNOT d2 a1
MEASURE 3 -> c[0]
MEASURE 4 -> c[1]

// Correct based on the syndrome: 00=no error, 01=d0, 11=d1, 10=d2.
SWITCH c {
CASE 0: BARRIER
CASE 1: X 0
CASE 2: X 2
CASE 3: X 1
}

MEASURE 0
MEASURE 1
MEASURE 2

// Verified against the real simulator for all 4 cases (no error, error on
// d0, d1, d2 — insert "X 0"/"X 1"/"X 2" before the syndrome section above):
// every case ends with all 3 data qubits back at |1>, and the syndrome bits
// correctly identify which qubit (if any) flipped.
//
// quell run qec_bitflip.quell
